earned man uh there's been a  lot of comparison between a partygate  fine and a parking fine being comparable  um is it  no it's not uh and we have to remember  that this you know let's go back to the  point at which these offences uh may  have occurred at that point the  government including the prime minister  was stressing that this was of very  great importance that we all needed to  play our part that this was all about  you know protecting lives and that's why  those rules were in place so i think to  dismiss these as as as minor issues  given  the nature of the  lockdown rules  given that the public messages that were  coming from the government at the time  given that it was the prime minister  himself who decided to impose these  restrictions  i think the idea that he should just be  dismissed as as very minor offenses are  not really significant  and also  there is this issue here of what the  prime minister has said to parliament  and the accusation of uh misleading  parliament now it doesn't matter you  know if the prime minister had stood up  and said you know i've never  committed a parking offense and he had  that would be a serious matter because  you you just can't mislead parliament  he says as you well know and i'll put it  to you again that on the two occasions  that he allegedly misled parliament  at that time that's what he believed and  we can't disprove that we don't know  what was in his head at the time  well i think that's  the argument about whether he has  mislead parliament or not and that is  what the privileged privileges committee  uh committee made up of members of  parliament should investigate um i i  think there comes a point  maybe maybe it doesn't apply in the case  of the  birthday party uh but there comes a  point where if it is so obvious that it  is a party where there is so much  evidence that there are drinks there  that there are nibbles there maybe music  is playing yeah we don't entirely know  we don't have all the information yet um  but if the evidence is so  clear that in fact you know it looked  like a party it sounded like a party  um then you know it is a party and it's  not credible for the prime minister to  claim otherwise and the thought  otherwise but that's that's for the  committee to investigate and i think it  is right that it investigates that  specifically focused on the issue of  whether he has mislead parliament as  well but isn't the point  one that you just touched on there that  we don't know yet what all the evidence  is so why are we um hang drawn and  quartering him when we don't actually  know what either the police or sue gray  have concluded  because the this uh this motion that the  labour party has put forward isn't  calling for him to be  hanged drawn or quartered um what it's  saying is there should be an  investigation i think there is enough  evidence there  given the police's conclusion in terms  of uh the the the so-called birthday  party that there's a need for an  investigation  and what conclusions should be reached  should be based on the evidence and as i  say we're not talking about this  investigation commencing until the  police process has been completed  but i think it is reasonable at this  point for parliament to make it clear  that the evidence is sufficient that it  is of such seriousness that there should  be an investigation  but i agree the investigation shouldn't  start until the police have completed  their process  and the evidence can then be put before  the privileges committee  okay good to talk to you thank you very  much indeed for joining us thank you  thank you  now an investigation into a fatal  shooting on the set of the alec baldwin  movie rust has found that there were  willful and serious breaches of gun  safety rules on set baldwin was pointing  a gun at the cinematographer helena  hutchins while preparing for a scene  last october when it went off killing  her our us correspondent martha kellner  has this repor